  "usage": {
    "summary": "Decode CPU identity, ISA features, caches and raw CPUID records with a C library and command-line tool.",
    "locations": [
      {
        "label": "Commands",
        "path": "{prefix}/bin",
        "purpose": "Version-selected command-line tools; use these absolute paths"
      },
      {
        "label": "Libraries and headers",
        "path": "{prefix}",
        "purpose": "C API, static/shared libraries and pkg-config metadata"
      },
      {
        "label": "Original source",
        "path": "{assets}/sources/libcpuid",
        "purpose": "Complete upstream source expanded only by a source installation. Binary installations retain the source lock and can acquire the separately published source package."
      },
      {
        "label": "License and source usage",
        "path": "{prefix}/share/doc/libcpuid",
        "purpose": "Upstream license, README and the installed API-check source"
      },
      {
        "label": "Fresh C consumer check",
        "path": "{prefix}/share/compiler-playground/libcpuid/consumer_check.py",
        "purpose": "Compiles shared and static clients using only this installation\u2019s headers, library and pkg-config metadata."
      },
      {
        "label": "Official decoding fixtures",
        "path": "{prefix}/share/compiler-playground/libcpuid/upstream-tests",
        "purpose": "All 501 upstream expected-output fixtures and the unmodified official test runner; never regenerate expected outputs during qualification."
      }
    ],
    "activation": [
      "Use the resolved absolute command paths shown here. No system-wide PATH or alternatives changes are made."
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "description": "Decode the currently visible CPU without timing or MSR access",
        "command": "{prefix}/bin/cpuid_tool --report"
      },
      {
        "description": "Save raw CPU data for reproducible offline decoding",
        "command": "{prefix}/bin/cpuid_tool --save=/tmp/cpu.raw.txt --report"
      },
      {
        "description": "Replay a saved identity record",
        "command": "{prefix}/bin/cpuid_tool --load=/tmp/cpu.raw.txt --report"
      },
      {
        "description": "Compile fresh consumers from the installed API example",
        "command": "python3 {prefix}/share/compiler-playground/libcpuid/consumer_check.py --prefix {prefix}"
      },
      {
        "description": "Run the official decoder fixture suite on this installation",
        "command": "python3 {prefix}/share/compiler-playground/libcpuid/upstream_check.py --prefix {prefix}"
      }
    ],
    "notes": [
      "Basic CPU identification needs no root or MSR access. Optional --rdmsr and clock functions are separate hardware operations and are not part of deployment checks.",
      "A container or hypervisor exposes a CPU view. Identification does not establish host frequency, available PMU access, or compiler performance.",
      "The source package includes all upstream CPU identification fixtures. Native verification passed all 501 fixtures for 0.8.2.",
      "The APT cpuid command and libcpuid cpuid_tool are distinct projects and can be selected together.",
      "Each installed ELF tool uses a package-local launcher to load its own shared library. The launcher does not alter the parent environment.",
      "A prebuilt package must record architecture, libc, compiler and build profile. Native build evidence is not an OCI image or arbitrary-platform qualification.",
      "pkg-config metadata uses its containing prefix; legacy libtool .la files retain build-prefix metadata and are not advertised as relocatable link inputs.",
      "Build 2 additionally qualifies fresh shared/static consumers and verifies that the dynamic API probe resolves the library in this exact prefix. Hardware counters, MSR access and frequency measurement are not enabled by these checks."
    ],
    "documentation": [
      "https://github.com/anrieff/libcpuid/releases/tag/v0.8.2",
      "https://anrieff.github.io/libcpuid/"
    ]
  },
